There is a PATCH form that applies JSON Patch RFC 6902 to the document
as a whole. A top level series of these is equivalent to a RFC-6902
document.

(patch replace
    (path "/FirstName")
    (value "Jane"))

The six RFC 6902 operation names are represented in the second position
as case-insensitive bare-words: ADD, REMOVE, REPLACE, MOVE, COPY, TEST.

Similarly, a MERGE form exists that applies JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396)
semantics.

(merge (object
    (title . "Hello!")
    (phoneNumber . "+01-123-456-7890")
    (author . (object (familyName . NULL)))
    (tags . (array "example"))))

For convenience, an escape form that accepts exactly one JSON value
between JSON ... END delimiters is accepted. Note that this can be
applied in any VALUE position for either JSON Patch or JSON Merge Patch
operations.

(merge (JSON
    {
        "title": "Hello!",
        "phoneNumber": "+01-123-456-7890",
        "author": {
            "familyName": null
        },
        "tags": [ "example" ]
    }
END))

Last, a QUERY form is provided that allows the specification of JSON
Path queries (RFC 9535) and the application of either JSON Patch or JSON
Merge Patch operations to the results:

(query "$..[?(@.sharedKey == true)]"
    (patch remove (path "/sharedKey")))

Queries can be nested within query forms, but never within merge or
patch forms. In other words, (query ... (query ... (merge ...))) would
be valid forms but (query ... (merge ... (query ...))) would not be.

Within each nested query form, `$` refers to the currently selected
subtree.

Each query accepts exactly one body form: a patch, a merge, or nested
query.

There are two value-construction forms, ARRAY and OBJECT, that allow the
specification of JSON values of those types. There is also a global
constant NULL that represents JSON null.
